  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts earlier light-driven biohybrid systems with the strategy introduced here. In the upper half, previous studies use light to excite a photocatalyst, generating electrons and protons that feed into a microbial cell. Those electrons seem to support cofactor formation such as NADH/ATP, mainly leading to sugar, simple chemicals, or some complex chemicals after intermediate, partly unknown steps.\n\nThe lower half presents a redesigned biohybrid interface built around “CC NPLs,” which are illuminated to produce many photoelectrons. A key visible advance is the use of “TPP” as a newly identified mediator that appears to shuttle these electrons into the cell more effectively. The incoming reducing power drives larger cofactor pools, shown as enlarged NADH/ATP-like nodes, which then support biosynthesis from waste carbon rather than from sugar. The outputs are higher-value complex chemicals, with examples illustrated as BDO, PHB, and aircraft fuel.\n\nOverall, the message is that this work improves solar-to-biochemical conversion by engineering the photocatalyst–microbe interface, discovering a mediator for electron transfer, and channeling light energy plus waste carbon into more valuable chemical products.",
